<p>British Health Secretary Wes Streeting resigned from Prime Minister Keir Starmer’s government on Thursday, saying he had “lost confidence” in Starmer’s leadership and calling for a Labour leadership contest after poor local and regional election results last week. Streeting became the first Cabinet member to resign during the challenge to Starmer and said in an open letter that it was “clear” Starmer would not lead Labour into the next general election. Streeting stopped short of immediately launching his own leadership bid, leaving unclear whether he had enough Labour MPs to trigger a contest. Starmer, whose party won a large parliamentary majority less than two years ago, has said he intends to remain in office despite more than 90 Labour MPs calling for him to go.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/cM40HB2805zfKxN6BTZMakoOaQMaVWasvVFCFvqzzQ3id61n898DHUvDFjI-c-ghjspieuag9GF_78tvb6TN4W_m8ezsAf1iaqkPcqdQJDHlzrJEsJ4nojV4HFns96817EILI_NonEo1nN8tR2Ccp5g8CCvH-L4nBnrdpXcgk7w4jd3151zyfnr2RecOV7l8M50n-IT3gcPZ' alt='Wes Streeting walking on Downing Street, London, where he had a meeting with Prime Minister Keir Starmer, on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pre-resignation maneuvering: British media had reported for days that Streeting might quit, and a possible challenge was already overshadowing Starmer’s King’s Speech plans.</li><li>Election losses: Streeting cited Labour’s “unprecedented” 2026 local and devolved election results and said the government’s unpopularity was a “major factor” in the defeats.</li><li>Policy criticism: In his resignation letter, Streeting cited the winter fuel allowance decision and Starmer’s “island of strangers” speech as examples of policy and messaging mistakes.</li><li>Possible rivals: Reports identified Angela Rayner, Andy Burnham and defence minister Al Carns as figures linked to a possible contest or wider Labour leadership field.</li></ul><blockquote>Where we need vision, we have a vacuum. Where we need direction, 